<!doctype html>
<html lang="en" data-theme="dark">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
{{csp_meta}}
<title>Fraud Console · Fraud-E2E</title>
<meta name="description" content="A fraud-scoring console in the browser: a logistic model exported from the Python pipeline scores transactions live, with a cost-based step-up threshold, per-feature contributions and an uncertainty interval on every number.">
<meta name="author" content="Natpaphon Nukhunthod">
<meta name="color-scheme" content="dark light">
<meta name="theme-color" content="#0a0c0f">
<meta property="og:type" content="website">
<meta property="og:title" content="Fraud Console · cost-aware credit card fraud detection">
<meta property="og:description" content="Live in-browser scoring, a threshold simulator with illustrative costs, and a pre-registered evaluation on a public CC0 dataset.">
{{canonical_html}}
<link rel="icon" href="favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
<script src="theme.js"></script>
<script type="module" src="app.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
<!-- Generated by scripts/build_web.py from web/src/ and reports/. Do not edit; edit web/src/ and run `make docs`. -->
<a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>

<header class="topbar">
  <div class="bar">
    <a class="brand" href="#top" aria-label="Fraud-E2E console, back to top"><span class="brand-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>FRAUD-E2E<span class="brand-sub">/console</span></a>
    <ul class="flags" aria-label="Important caveats">
      <li class="flag">Public CC0 dataset of unspecified provenance</li>
      <li class="flag flag--cost">{{cost_label}}</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="bar-actions">
      <button type="button" class="cmd" id="open-palette" aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-keyshortcuts="Meta+K Control+K">
        <span class="cmd-label">Jump to</span><kbd id="cmd-key">Ctrl K</kbd></button>
      <button type="button" class="icon-btn" id="theme-toggle" aria-pressed="false" title="Toggle light mode (T)">
        <span class="sr-only">Light mode</span><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="8" cy="8" r="6.25"/><path d="M8 1.75a6.25 6.25 0 0 1 0 12.5z"/></svg></button>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<main id="main">

<section class="hero" id="top" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="hero-head">
      <p class="kicker">credit card fraud · cost-aware step-up · {{final_model}}</p>
      <h1 id="hero-title">Every transaction, scored live in your browser.</h1>
      <p class="hero-sub">The model below was exported from the Python pipeline and runs here. It scores dev
        transactions it was never trained on. Scores at or above the threshold get <b class="tag tag--step">STEP-UP</b>;
        the rest <b class="tag">APPROVE</b>.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="console">
      <div class="panel stream" id="stream" aria-labelledby="stream-title">
        <div class="panel-head">
          <h2 class="panel-title" id="stream-title"><span class="live" id="live-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>Live stream</h2>
          <div class="controls" role="group" aria-label="Stream controls">
            <button type="button" id="s-play" class="btn" aria-keyshortcuts="P" disabled>Pause</button>
            <button type="button" id="s-step" class="btn" aria-keyshortcuts="." disabled>Step</button>
            <div class="seg" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Speed">
              <button type="button" role="radio" data-speed="1" aria-checked="true" disabled>1×</button>
              <button type="button" role="radio" data-speed="4" aria-checked="false" disabled>4×</button>
              <button type="button" role="radio" data-speed="16" aria-checked="false" disabled>16×</button>
            </div>
            <button type="button" id="s-restart" class="btn btn--ghost" disabled>Restart</button>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="panel-meta">{{stream_n}} dev transactions ({{stream_fraud}} fraud), random order · each scored
          out-of-fold by the CV model that did not train on it · <span id="s-progress">0 played</span></p>
        <p class="legend-line" id="s-legend"><span class="lg-item"><span class="o o--tp">●</span>fraud caught</span>
          <span class="lg-item"><span class="o o--fn">×</span>fraud missed</span>
          <span class="lg-item"><span class="o o--fp">○</span>false alarm</span>
          <span class="lg-item"><span class="o o--tn">·</span>legit approved</span>
          <span class="lg-item lg-bar"><span class="lg-swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span>score bar: log scale, 0.0001 → 1; tick = threshold</span></p>
        <div class="srow srow--head" aria-hidden="true">
          <span>txn</span><span class="r">amount</span><span class="c-merch">merchant</span><span class="c-chan">channel</span><span title="Bar on a log scale from 0.0001 to 1; the tick marks the threshold">score <span class="c-scale">· log scale</span><span class="c-scale-s">(log)</span></span><span>decision</span><span class="c-out">truth</span>
        </div>
        <ol class="stream-rows" id="s-rows" aria-label="Most recent transactions, newest first"></ol>
        <p class="stream-hint" id="s-hint" hidden></p>
        <p class="fine stream-foot">Select a row to inspect it.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="side">
        <div class="panel counters" aria-labelledby="ctr-title">
          <div class="panel-head">
            <h2 class="panel-title" id="ctr-title">Outcomes</h2>
          </div>
          <label class="thr-mini" for="thr-mini">
            <span class="k">threshold</span><output class="v" id="thr-mini-out" for="thr-mini">{{threshold}}</output>
          </label>
          <input type="range" id="thr-mini" min="0" max="1" step="1" value="0" aria-label="Step-up threshold (shared with the simulator)" disabled>

          <div class="ctr-block" aria-labelledby="full-title">
            <h3 class="ctr-title" id="full-title"><span class="scope scope--full">full sample</span>
              <span class="ctr-meta">all {{stream_n}} stream rows · {{stream_fraud}} fraud</span></h3>
            <p class="fine scope-note">Demo sample of {{stream_n}} dev rows ({{stream_fraud}} fraud), not the model
              evaluation &mdash; see <a href="#metrics">Model, measured</a> for CV and holdout.</p>
            <dl class="ctr-grid ctr-grid--2">
              <div><dt>recall</dt><dd id="f-recall">—</dd></div>
              <div><dt>precision</dt><dd id="f-precision">—</dd></div>
              <div><dt>stepped up</dt><dd id="f-alert">—</dd></div>
              <div><dt>cost / 1,000 txn*</dt><dd id="f-cost">—</dd></div>
            </dl>
            <p class="fine" id="f-counts"></p>
          </div>

          <div class="ctr-block" aria-labelledby="played-title">
            <h3 class="ctr-title" id="played-title"><span class="scope">played so far</span>
              <span class="ctr-meta" id="c-played">0 / {{stream_n}} rows</span></h3>
            <dl class="ctr-grid">
              <div><dt>caught</dt><dd id="c-tp">0</dd></div>
              <div><dt>missed</dt><dd id="c-fn">0</dd></div>
              <div><dt>false alarms</dt><dd id="c-fp">0</dd></div>
              <div><dt>approved ok</dt><dd id="c-tn">0</dd></div>
              <div><dt>recall</dt><dd id="c-recall">—</dd></div>
              <div><dt>precision</dt><dd id="c-precision">—</dd></div>
            </dl>
          </div>
          <p class="fine">Both use the same out-of-fold scores and the current threshold; moving it re-labels every
            row. Rates on the rows played so far are noisy until many rows have played. *{{cost_label}}, as set in the
            simulator below.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="panel inspector" id="inspector" aria-labelledby="insp-title">
          <div class="panel-head">
            <h2 class="panel-title" id="insp-title">Inspector</h2>
            <span class="pill" id="insp-mode">following live</span>
          </div>
          <div id="insp-body"><p class="fine">Waiting for the first transaction&hellip;</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <noscript><p class="fine">The live console needs JavaScript. The results, simulator defaults and report
      below are readable without it.</p></noscript>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap block" id="metrics" aria-labelledby="metrics-title">
  <div class="block-head"><span class="idx">01</span><h2 id="metrics-title">Model, measured</h2>
    <p class="block-sub">{{final_model}} · holdout scored once · random score &asymp; {{ho_noskill}}</p></div>
  <ul class="stats">
    <li><span class="k">PR-AUC · holdout</span><span class="v">{{ho_pr_auc}}</span><span class="ci">95% CI {{ho_pr_auc_ci}}</span></li>
    <li><span class="k">ROC-AUC · holdout</span><span class="v">{{ho_roc_auc}}</span><span class="ci">95% CI {{ho_roc_auc_ci}}</span></li>
    <li><span class="k">fraud caught · holdout</span><span class="v">{{ho_recall}}</span><span class="ci">95% CI {{ho_recall_ci}} · {{ho_alert_rate}} stepped up</span></li>
    <li><span class="k">cost cut vs nobody · CV</span><span class="v">{{cv_saving}}</span><span class="ci">illustrative costs · holdout {{ho_saving}} {{ho_saving_ci}}, post-hoc</span></li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="wrap block" id="simulator" aria-labelledby="sim-title">
  <div class="block-head"><span class="idx">02</span><h2 id="sim-title">Threshold &amp; cost</h2>
    <p class="block-sub">Recomputed in your browser from out-of-fold predictions on the dev split ({{dev_n}} transactions,
      {{dev_fraud}} fraud; counts averaged over the 3 CV repeats). The holdout is not used here. The live stream uses
      the same threshold.</p></div>
  <p class="callout" role="note"><b>{{cost_label}}.</b> The defaults are assumptions made for this project. With a
    bank&rsquo;s real costs the best threshold would have to be re-derived; scaling each cost &times;0.5 or &times;2
    moves it between {{sens_thr_min}} and {{sens_thr_max}}.</p>

  <div class="sim" id="sim">
    <form class="sim-controls" id="sim-form" autocomplete="off">
      <div class="panel field--threshold">
        <label for="thr" class="thr-label"><span class="k">step-up threshold</span> <output id="thr-out" for="thr">{{threshold}}</output></label>
        <input type="range" id="thr" name="thr" min="0" max="1" step="1" value="0" disabled aria-describedby="thr-help">
        <p class="fine" id="thr-help">Transactions scoring at or above this are stepped up. The slider moves along the
          threshold grid (roughly logarithmic).</p>
        <div class="buttons">
          <button type="button" class="btn" id="btn-best" disabled>Lowest cost for these costs</button>
          <button type="button" class="btn btn--ghost" id="btn-project" disabled>Project choice ({{threshold}})</button>
        </div>
        <p class="fine best-note" id="o-best"></p>
      </div>
      <fieldset class="panel costs">
        <legend><span class="k">cost assumptions</span> <span class="muted">illustrative · USD</span></legend>
        <label class="field">Review / step-up cost per flagged transaction
          <input type="number" id="c-review" name="review_cost_per_alert" min="0" step="1" value="{{cost_review}}" inputmode="decimal" disabled></label>
        <label class="field">Extra friction when a good customer is stepped up
          <input type="number" id="c-friction" name="fp_friction_cost" min="0" step="1" value="{{cost_friction}}" inputmode="decimal" disabled></label>
        <label class="field">Share of a missed fraud&rsquo;s amount that is lost (0&ndash;2)
          <input type="number" id="c-fnfrac" name="fn_loss_fraction" min="0" max="2" step="0.1" value="{{cost_fn_frac}}" inputmode="decimal" disabled></label>
        <label class="field">Fixed handling cost per missed fraud
          <input type="number" id="c-fnfixed" name="fn_fixed_cost" min="0" step="1" value="{{cost_fn_fixed}}" inputmode="decimal" disabled></label>
        <button type="reset" class="btn btn--ghost" id="btn-reset" disabled>Reset to project defaults</button>
        <p class="error" id="cost-error" role="alert" hidden></p>
      </fieldset>
    </form>

    <div class="sim-out">
      <ul class="stats stats--sim" aria-live="polite">
        <li><span class="k">fraud caught (recall)</span><span class="v" id="o-recall">{{cv_recall}}</span></li>
        <li><span class="k">step-ups that are fraud</span><span class="v" id="o-precision">{{cv_precision}}</span></li>
        <li><span class="k">transactions stepped up</span><span class="v" id="o-alert">{{cv_alert_rate}}</span></li>
        <li><span class="k">expected cost / 1,000 txn</span><span class="v" id="o-cost">{{cv_cost_per_1000}}</span>
          <span class="ci" id="o-cost-sub">vs {{cv_nothing_per_1000}} stepping up nobody ({{cv_saving}} saved)</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="fine sim-sentence" id="o-sentence"></p>
      <figure class="panel chart">
        <figcaption><span class="k">expected cost per 1,000 transactions vs threshold</span> <span class="muted">USD ·
          log threshold axis · click to set</span></figcaption>
        <div class="chart-box" id="cost-chart"></div>
        <div class="legend" aria-hidden="true">
          <span><i class="key key--line"></i>model + threshold</span>
          <span><i class="key key--ref"></i>step up nobody</span>
          <span><i class="key key--now"></i>current</span>
          <span><i class="key key--best"></i>lowest cost</span>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap block" id="playground" aria-labelledby="pg-title">
  <div class="block-head"><span class="idx">03</span><h2 id="pg-title">Try it yourself</h2>
    <p class="block-sub">Build a transaction and watch the score and the decision change. Scored by the final model
      (refit on all {{dev_n}} dev rows, the model that was scored once on the holdout), at the threshold set above.</p></div>
  <div class="pg">
    <form class="panel pg-form" id="pg-form" autocomplete="off" aria-label="Transaction builder">
      <div class="pg-presets" role="group" aria-label="Load a transaction">
        <span class="k">load</span>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn--ghost" data-preset="fraud" disabled>random fraud</button>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn--ghost" data-preset="legit" disabled>random legit</button>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn--ghost" data-preset="inspector" disabled>from inspector</button>
      </div>
      <div class="pg-fields" id="pg-fields"></div>
    </form>
    <div class="panel pg-out" aria-live="polite">
      <div class="pg-score">
        <span class="k">score</span><span class="v" id="pg-score">—</span>
        <span class="decision" id="pg-decision">—</span>
      </div>
      <div class="gauge" id="pg-gauge" aria-hidden="true"><span class="gauge-thr"></span><span class="gauge-dot"></span></div>
      <p class="fine" id="pg-note"></p>
      <div id="pg-contrib"></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap block report" id="report" aria-labelledby="report-title">
  <div class="block-head"><span class="idx">04</span><h2 id="report-title">Report</h2>
    <p class="block-sub">The full write-up, folded. Every number is generated from the project&rsquo;s committed result
      files; nothing is typed by hand.</p></div>

  <details class="module" id="r-problem">
    <summary><span class="m-idx">04.1</span><span class="m-title">The problem</span><span class="m-sub">rank fraud well, then pick the cheapest threshold</span></summary>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>A card issuer scores every transaction. Scores above a threshold go to <strong>step-up</strong> (manual review
        or extra authentication such as OTP / 3-D Secure); the rest are approved. Two mistakes cost money in different
        ways: a good customer stepped up (friction and review effort) and a missed fraud (the amount is lost).</p>
      <p>So the goal has two parts: a model that <strong>ranks</strong> fraud well, measured by PR-AUC because only
        {{fraud_rate}} of transactions are fraud, and a <strong>threshold</strong> that minimises total expected cost
        rather than one that maximises an accuracy-style metric.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="module" id="r-data">
    <summary><span class="m-idx">04.2</span><span class="m-title">Data and its limits</span><span class="m-sub">{{n_rows}} rows · {{n_fraud}} fraud · unspecified provenance</span></summary>
    <div class="prose">
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/uditjain13/credit-card-fraud-detection-2026"><em>Credit Card Fraud
          Detection 2026</em></a> by Udit Jain on Kaggle, released under <strong>CC0</strong>. It is a <strong>public
          dataset of unspecified provenance</strong>: the data card does not say whether rows are real or synthetic, so
          no result here is a claim about real-world card fraud.</li>
        <li>{{n_rows}} transactions, {{n_fraud}} of them fraud ({{fraud_rate}}, 95% CI {{fraud_rate_ci}}),
          {{n_features}} features. {{n_checks_failed}} of {{n_checks}} structural data checks failed.</li>
        <li>Hints of synthetic generation: channel and device look statistically independent (p = {{chan_dev_p}}),
          and the smallest fraud is {{fraud_min_usd}}.</li>
        <li><strong>No customer or card ID and no real timestamp</strong>, so no customer-history features, no grouped
          split and no out-of-time test.</li>
        <li>The split was locked before any label-based analysis: a development set of {{dev_n}} rows ({{dev_fraud}}
          fraud) for 5-fold cross-validation repeated 3 times, and a holdout of {{holdout_n}} rows ({{holdout_fraud}}
          fraud) scored <strong>once</strong> at the end.</li>
        <li>The live stream above is a random sample of {{stream_n}} dev rows ({{stream_fraud}} fraud); it contains no
          holdout rows.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="module" id="r-results">
    <summary><span class="m-idx">04.3</span><span class="m-title">Main results</span><span class="m-sub">logistic beat boosting: {{boost_vs_lr_diff}} {{boost_vs_lr_ci}}</span></summary>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Final model: <strong>{{final_model}}</strong>. A random score has PR-AUC &asymp; {{noskill}} (the fraud rate);
        the model reaches about {{cv_lift}}&times; that on cross-validation.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="results">
          <caption>Cross-validation on the dev split vs the holdout, scored once. Brackets are 95% confidence intervals
            (CV: across 15 folds; holdout: stratified bootstrap).</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">metric</th><th scope="col">CV on dev</th><th scope="col">holdout ({{ho_fraud}} fraud)</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">PR-AUC (primary)</th><td>{{cv_pr_auc}} <span class="ci">{{cv_pr_auc_ci}}</span></td><td>{{ho_pr_auc}} <span class="ci">{{ho_pr_auc_ci}}</span></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">ROC-AUC</th><td>{{cv_roc_auc}} <span class="ci">{{cv_roc_auc_ci}}</span></td><td>{{ho_roc_auc}} <span class="ci">{{ho_roc_auc_ci}}</span></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Recall at threshold {{threshold}}</th><td>{{cv_recall}}</td><td>{{ho_recall}} <span class="ci">{{ho_recall_ci}}</span></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Precision at that threshold</th><td>{{cv_precision}}</td><td>{{ho_precision}} <span class="ci">{{ho_precision_ci}}</span></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Share stepped up</th><td>{{cv_alert_rate}}</td><td>{{ho_alert_rate}} <span class="ci">{{ho_alert_rate_ci}}</span></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>The holdout agrees with CV within its (wide) intervals. <strong>Logistic regression beat the best gradient
        boosting pipeline</strong> (<code>{{best_boost_name}}</code>, PR-AUC {{best_boost_pr_auc}}): boosting minus
        logistic = {{boost_vs_lr_diff}} {{boost_vs_lr_ci}}. On this dataset the fraud odds multiply with each extra red
        flag ({{flag_odds_multipliers}} per flag), the additive log-odds shape a logistic model represents directly.
        That is a statement about this data, not about fraud models in general.</p>
      <p>The step-up threshold {{threshold}} was chosen on dev out-of-fold predictions only (per-repeat optimum
        {{thr_repeat_range}}). On CV it catches {{cv_recall}} of fraud while stepping up {{cv_alert_rate}} of
        transactions, about {{alerts_per_fraud}} step-ups per fraud found.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="module" id="r-drivers">
    <summary><span class="m-idx">04.4</span><span class="m-title">What drives the score</span><span class="m-sub">top 3 features carry {{shap_top3_share}} of mean |SHAP|</span></summary>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Mean absolute SHAP value per feature on the dev split (log-odds units; exact linear SHAP for the logistic
        model, one-hot columns summed back to their source feature). The top three &mdash; {{shap_top3}} &mdash; carry
        {{shap_top3_share}} of the total.</p>
      {{shap_html}}
      <p>A false alarm and a caught fraud can carry the same red flags. Separating them more likely needs information
        this dataset does not have (customer history) than a different algorithm.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="module" id="r-didnt-work">
    <summary><span class="m-idx">04.5</span><span class="m-title">What didn&rsquo;t work</span><span class="m-sub">SMOTE {{smote_diff}} · decline tier not distinguishable on holdout</span></summary>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Every comparison was pre-registered: the simpler option is kept unless the 95% CI of the paired difference
        (corrected resampled t-test over 15 folds) excludes zero.</p>
      {{forest_html}}
      <ul>
        <li><strong>SMOTE hurt badly</strong> ({{smote_diff}} {{smote_ci}}). SMOTENC fills categorical and boolean
          features by neighbour majority vote, so synthetic fraud carried red flags only {{smote_flag_ratio}}&times; as
          often as real fraud (IP-country mismatch: {{smote_ipmis_real}} of real fraud vs {{smote_ipmis_syn}} of
          synthetic).</li>
        <li><strong>Class weights and undersampling</strong> were not distinguishable from doing nothing.</li>
        <li><strong>Calibration:</strong> {{calibration_verdict}}.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="note">
        <h3>A third tier (&ldquo;decline&rdquo;) did not clearly pay off</h3>
        <p>Approve / step-up / decline, with thresholds {{tt_stepup}} and {{tt_decline}} chosen on CV, looked cheaper
          on CV ({{tt_cv_diff}} per 1,000 transactions vs two tiers with the same model). On the holdout the difference
          was {{ho_tt_diff}} {{ho_tt_diff_ci}} per 1,000: the interval includes zero, so the simpler two-tier policy is
          recommended. <span class="muted">Assumes {{tt_pass_rate_pct}} of fraudsters pass step-up and a wrongly
          declined good customer costs {{tt_decline_fp}} USD. {{cost_label}}.</span></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="module" id="r-method">
    <summary><span class="m-idx">04.6</span><span class="m-title">How the evaluation stays honest</span><span class="m-sub">locked holdout · pre-registration · tested</span></summary>
    <div class="prose">
      <ul>
        <li>Holdout locked before any label-based analysis and scored once; a lock file refuses any other pipeline or
          threshold.</li>
        <li>All preprocessing and resampling are fitted inside training folds; leakage guards run in the test suite.</li>
        <li>Comparisons, metrics and decision rules were written down before the results (pre-registration with dated
          amendments).</li>
        <li>The in-browser model is the Python pipeline exported as coefficients, one-hot categories and scaler
          parameters; a test checks that the browser&rsquo;s score for every transaction on this page matches Python
          to within 1e-9.</li>
        <li>Every number on this page is generated from the project&rsquo;s committed result files; nothing is typed by
          hand.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>

</main>

<footer class="footer" id="credits">
  <div class="wrap">
    {{links_html}}
    <p><span class="k">data</span> <a href="https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/uditjain13/credit-card-fraud-detection-2026">Credit
      Card Fraud Detection 2026</a> by Udit Jain, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/">CC0 1.0
      (public domain)</a>. A public dataset of unspecified provenance; nothing here describes a real bank&rsquo;s
      customers.</p>
    <p><span class="k">costs</span> {{cost_label}}.</p>
    <p class="muted">A portfolio project by Natpaphon Nukhunthod, not a production system and not advice. Data file
      sha256 <code>{{data_sha_short}}</code>&hellip; · model <code>{{final_spec_id}}</code> · built from
      <code>reports/</code> by <code>scripts/build_web.py</code>.</p>
  </div>
</footer>

<dialog class="palette" id="palette" aria-label="Command palette">
  <div class="pal-in">
    <input type="text" id="pal-input" role="combobox" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="pal-list"
           aria-autocomplete="list" placeholder="Jump to a section or run a command…" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false">
    <ul id="pal-list" role="listbox" aria-label="Commands"></ul>
    <p class="pal-foot"><kbd>↑</kbd><kbd>↓</kbd> move · <kbd>↵</kbd> run · <kbd>esc</kbd> close</p>
  </div>
</dialog>

<script type="application/json" id="site-data">{{data_json}}</script>
</body>
</html>
